## The brief

There wasn't one single brief. Requests came in from the product, sales and marketing teams, each with its own deadline, and everything had to follow the existing DaoLens brand guideline. The requests ranged from Instagram and Twitter posts to a YouTube banner, website banners, Discord emoticons, a Discord onboarding animation, a handbook for Grape DAO and small changes to the website UI.

## My role

My responsibilities were to:

- Design the graphics each team asked for
- Stay within the brand guidelines and deliver on time
- Retouch and manipulate images where a design needed it
- Take feedback from the graphic design consultant and others involved, and revise

## What I made

### Social media

The largest share of my time went to social content. For Instagram I made thumbnails and posts, many of them framed as questions about DAOs, such as "Why DAOs?", "Is a DAO like a bank?" and "What is conviction voting?". Each used a bold headline with one highlighted word, sitting on the brand's purple and blue gradients.

![A grid of Instagram thumbnails and posts](/work/daolens/instagram-posts.webp "Instagram thumbnails and posts")

For Twitter and LinkedIn I designed product feed graphics, a Twitter Space card for "Can a DAO run its own city?", and partnership and grant announcements, including one for an Aave Grants DAO grant.

![Twitter and LinkedIn posts, a Twitter Space card and partnership announcements](/work/daolens/social-posts-and-announcements.webp "Product feed graphics, a Twitter Space card and announcements")

### Discord

DaoLens ran its community on Discord, so a lot of the work lived there. I made a set of emoticons in the brand's colours: a +1, an upvote arrow, a checkmark on the DaoLens mark, and a few characters, including a panda and a dinosaur giving a thumbs up.

![The Discord emoticon set](/work/daolens/discord-emoticons.webp "Discord emoticons")

I also worked on the Discord onboarding animation. A welcome screen greets the new member, and a congratulations sequence plays once they finish onboarding. The scene uses the DaoLens mark as a lit doorway at the top of a staircase, with floating puzzle pieces.

![Welcome screen and congratulations still frames](/work/daolens/discord-onboarding-animation.webp "Discord onboarding animation: welcome screen and congrats still frames")

To explain the onboarding tool itself, I made a poster covering its six features: gating, role assignment, a user engagement assistant, MetaMask login, analytics and setting the context.

### Banners

I designed the YouTube channel banners, a set of Twitter banners and banners for the website and a Reddit r/dao promotion. For the Twitter banner I tried three directions before settling on "Simplifying DAOs", which reused the doorway and staircase scene from the onboarding animation.

![Two YouTube banners, "Join the community" and "DAO manager"](/work/daolens/youtube-banners.webp "YouTube banners")

