## Process & decisions

Most requests were small and quick, so the brand guideline was what held them together. I stayed with the same purple-to-blue gradients, type and layout habits, so that posts, banners and decks made for different teams still looked like one company.

Over time a few visual motifs came back across different pieces. The DaoLens mark turned into a glowing doorway, staircases and puzzle pieces stood for onboarding, and the same characters showed up in emoticons and banners. Reusing them kept the pieces feeling connected, even when they were made weeks apart for different teams.

{% callout %}
With requests coming from three teams, I learned to prioritise the work and finish each piece in time for its deadline.
{% /callout %}

Feedback came from the graphic design consultant and the people who asked for each piece, and I revised the work based on it. Image retouching and manipulation was part of many jobs too, especially the posts built around photos of speakers.

## Outcome & learnings

Over five months I delivered work across fifteen kinds of requests, from single social posts to a full handbook and a deck. I don't have numbers on how the work performed, so I'll keep to what I took away from it:

- Feedback and talking with people across teams made the work better, and faster to get right.
- Handling several projects at once comes down to deciding what matters most and finishing it before the deadline.
- It was my first chance to use my skills on real, shipping work for a company rather than on coursework.
